1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:00,583
DAWN SONG: Hi.

2
00:00:00,583 --> 00:00:02,960
Thanks, everyone, for joining.

3
00:00:02,960 --> 00:00:08,240
So this is the final lecture for our MOOC this semester

4
00:00:08,240 --> 00:00:10,230
on advanced agents.

5
00:00:10,230 --> 00:00:11,430
My name is Dawn Song.

6
00:00:11,430 --> 00:00:14,030
I'm a professor in computer science at UC Berkeley

7
00:00:14,030 --> 00:00:17,180
and co-instructor for this MOOC.

8
00:00:17,180 --> 00:00:20,060
So today we will talk about towards building

9
00:00:20,060 --> 00:00:22,105
safe and secure agentic AI.

10
00:00:25,190 --> 00:00:27,830
And we've all been really excited

11
00:00:27,830 --> 00:00:31,080
about the fast advancement in frontier AI.

12
00:00:31,080 --> 00:00:35,930
And also, this year has been called the year of agents.

13
00:00:35,930 --> 00:00:39,800
And we've seen great advancements

14
00:00:39,800 --> 00:00:42,470
of various different types of agents,

15
00:00:42,470 --> 00:00:47,720
including web agents, computer use, coding agents,

16
00:00:47,720 --> 00:00:51,290
and even in robotics.

17
00:00:51,290 --> 00:00:56,120
On the one hand, we see the exciting advancement

18
00:00:56,120 --> 00:01:00,630
and deployment for frontier AI and AI agents.

19
00:01:00,630 --> 00:01:04,730
On the other hand, we also need to pay attention

20
00:01:04,730 --> 00:01:09,140
to a broad spectrum of different types of AI risks.

21
00:01:09,140 --> 00:01:13,190
So here is several different categories of AI

22
00:01:13,190 --> 00:01:18,000
risks, as listed in this international AI safety report,

23
00:01:18,000 --> 00:01:22,100
led by Turing Award winner Yoshua Bengio

24
00:01:22,100 --> 00:01:30,080
and written in collaboration by about 100 different leading AI

25
00:01:30,080 --> 00:01:32,580
researchers from 30 countries.

26
00:01:32,580 --> 00:01:35,155
And I'm part of these efforts as well.

27
00:01:37,880 --> 00:01:44,010
And also, I want to mention that as we deploy AI,

28
00:01:44,010 --> 00:01:47,340
it's also important to consider AI in the presence of attackers.

29
00:01:47,340 --> 00:01:51,200
One, history has shown that attackers always

30
00:01:51,200 --> 00:01:54,350
follow the footsteps of new technology development,

31
00:01:54,350 --> 00:01:55,880
sometimes even leads it.

32
00:01:55,880 --> 00:01:59,990
And also, this time, the stake is even higher with AI,

33
00:01:59,990 --> 00:02:02,450
as AI controls more and more systems,

34
00:02:02,450 --> 00:02:05,600
attacker will have higher and higher incentives

35
00:02:05,600 --> 00:02:07,680
to compromise these systems.

36
00:02:07,680 --> 00:02:10,289
And also, as AI becomes more and more capable,

37
00:02:10,289 --> 00:02:13,250
the consequences of misuse by attackers

38
00:02:13,250 --> 00:02:15,660
will also become more and more severe.

39
00:02:15,660 --> 00:02:19,010
And hence, it's important to consider safe and responsible AI

40
00:02:19,010 --> 00:02:22,490
in the adversarial setting.

41
00:02:22,490 --> 00:02:26,610
So we talk about AI safety and AI security,

42
00:02:26,610 --> 00:02:31,385
so here I want to just quickly introduce and clarify

43
00:02:31,385 --> 00:02:34,170
AI safety versus AI security.

44
00:02:34,170 --> 00:02:37,340
So the two are interrelated.

45
00:02:37,340 --> 00:02:42,020
AI safety is to prevent harm that a system might inflict

46
00:02:42,020 --> 00:02:44,150
upon the external environment.

47
00:02:44,150 --> 00:02:47,330
Whereas AI security is to protect the system itself

48
00:02:47,330 --> 00:02:49,520
against the harm and exploitation

49
00:02:49,520 --> 00:02:52,220
from malicious external actors.

50
00:02:52,220 --> 00:02:56,200
And AI safety needs to consider adversarial setting.

51
00:02:56,200 --> 00:02:59,760
So, for example, the alignment mechanisms

52
00:02:59,760 --> 00:03:04,470
need to be resilient and secure against attacks.

53
00:03:04,470 --> 00:03:08,490
And the overall goal is to advance safe and secure AI

54
00:03:08,490 --> 00:03:12,120
innovation to ensure its potential benefits are

55
00:03:12,120 --> 00:03:15,155
responsibly realized and widely shared.

56
00:03:17,880 --> 00:03:21,600
So in this lecture, I will talk about,

57
00:03:21,600 --> 00:03:26,230
in particular, safety and security issues in agentic AI.

58
00:03:26,230 --> 00:03:29,700
So first I'll give an overview for agentic AI safety

59
00:03:29,700 --> 00:03:36,430
and security problems and the problem definition, and so on.

60
00:03:36,430 --> 00:03:41,910
And then, I will talk about different types of attacks

61
00:03:41,910 --> 00:03:47,010
in agentic AI, and then how we can evaluate and do

62
00:03:47,010 --> 00:03:49,920
risk assessments in agentic AI, and then talk

63
00:03:49,920 --> 00:03:52,920
about defenses in agentic AI.

64
00:03:52,920 --> 00:03:54,810
And then finally, I also want to talk

65
00:03:54,810 --> 00:03:59,640
a little bit about the other side, how agentic AI can

66
00:03:59,640 --> 00:04:03,400
be misused, in particular in the domain of cybersecurity,

67
00:04:03,400 --> 00:04:06,030
and discuss the impact of frontier AI

68
00:04:06,030 --> 00:04:08,290
on the landscape of cybersecurity.

69
00:04:08,290 --> 00:04:13,740
And finally, I'll also briefly summarize our recent proposal

70
00:04:13,740 --> 00:04:18,690
on the path for science and evidence-based AI policy.

71
00:04:18,690 --> 00:04:25,230
So first, a overview for agentic AI safety and security.

72
00:04:25,230 --> 00:04:32,010
So first, I want to briefly contrast

73
00:04:32,010 --> 00:04:36,120
LLM safety versus agent safety.

74
00:04:36,120 --> 00:04:41,460
So in general so far, most of the applications

75
00:04:41,460 --> 00:04:43,990
that we interact with, such as chatbots and so on.

76
00:04:43,990 --> 00:04:47,460
They are mostly simple LLM applications

77
00:04:47,460 --> 00:04:51,600
where you take a text input and then

78
00:04:51,600 --> 00:04:56,560
the LLM will then process this input, also called prompt,

79
00:04:56,560 --> 00:04:58,960
to produce the text outputs.

80
00:04:58,960 --> 00:05:01,150
But when we talked about LLM agents,

81
00:05:01,150 --> 00:05:03,580
which is the focus of this course.

82
00:05:03,580 --> 00:05:09,100
The agent uses LLM as a critical core component,

83
00:05:09,100 --> 00:05:12,540
but the overall system is much more complex.

84
00:05:12,540 --> 00:05:17,370
The LLM components will observe from the environment

85
00:05:17,370 --> 00:05:22,030
and then get the observations.

86
00:05:22,030 --> 00:05:28,060
And will then use LLM to do reasoning and planning

87
00:05:28,060 --> 00:05:33,910
and may retrieve data from its memory and external database.

88
00:05:33,910 --> 00:05:39,820
And also, can then take action, including tool use and so on.

89
00:05:39,820 --> 00:05:46,230
And then the action will take place

90
00:05:46,230 --> 00:05:50,670
on the external environment.

91
00:05:50,670 --> 00:05:53,770
And then the environment can provide feedback.

92
00:05:53,770 --> 00:05:57,180
So overall, as you can see, the agent system

93
00:05:57,180 --> 00:06:02,280
is much more complex than the simple, like, chatbot example,

94
00:06:02,280 --> 00:06:08,920
where LLM is mainly used to take text inputs,

95
00:06:08,920 --> 00:06:11,130
or sometimes can take multi-modal inputs

96
00:06:11,130 --> 00:06:13,680
and produce the outputs.

97
00:06:13,680 --> 00:06:19,230
So first, even in such a simple case

98
00:06:19,230 --> 00:06:22,710
for when we just consider the simple model of LLM,

99
00:06:22,710 --> 00:06:25,920
taking inputs, producing outputs, already there

100
00:06:25,920 --> 00:06:31,920
are a lot of safety and security issues.

101
00:06:31,920 --> 00:06:36,240
Due to the interest of time, I won't actually

102
00:06:36,240 --> 00:06:40,920
go into details on safety in this lecture.

103
00:06:40,920 --> 00:06:46,080
So I just gave a keynote at ICLR, "I clear,"

104
00:06:46,080 --> 00:06:52,650
2025 in Singapore, which actually has over 12,000

105
00:06:52,650 --> 00:06:53,970
attendees.

106
00:06:53,970 --> 00:06:58,440
So in my keynote, I actually gave a broad overview

107
00:06:58,440 --> 00:07:05,200
on my journey and work and the overall space in safety.

108
00:07:05,200 --> 00:07:08,640
And you are welcome to watch the recording of my talk

109
00:07:08,640 --> 00:07:15,300
there to learn more about the different aspects of safety

110
00:07:15,300 --> 00:07:16,420
and security.

111
00:07:16,420 --> 00:07:18,880
So this, of course, is important.

112
00:07:18,880 --> 00:07:24,360
Like, for example, I myself, we need

113
00:07:24,360 --> 00:07:27,720
to ensure many different trustworthiness aspects,

114
00:07:27,720 --> 00:07:32,890
including privacy, memorization, adversarial robustness,

115
00:07:32,890 --> 00:07:38,940
fairness, and many-- a lot of different perspectives.

116
00:07:38,940 --> 00:07:43,950
So, of course, safety alone is a very important topic.

117
00:07:43,950 --> 00:07:48,850
But, however, when we talk about the agent safety and security,

118
00:07:48,850 --> 00:07:54,640
the issue is even more complex and can be even more severe

119
00:07:54,640 --> 00:07:59,480
in the sense that when we talk about safety in general,

120
00:07:59,480 --> 00:08:03,910
it's mostly that we want to ensure the outputs of the--

121
00:08:03,910 --> 00:08:08,950
satisfy certain safety, it doesn't cause harm.

122
00:08:08,950 --> 00:08:14,470
And, for example, it doesn't output toxic information

123
00:08:14,470 --> 00:08:18,100
and doesn't leak sensitive information and lots

124
00:08:18,100 --> 00:08:20,090
of other aspects, and so on.

125
00:08:20,090 --> 00:08:24,310
But in agents, the agent actually

126
00:08:24,310 --> 00:08:27,190
is going to take actions, and actions

127
00:08:27,190 --> 00:08:31,280
are going to have consequences on the external environment.

128
00:08:31,280 --> 00:08:36,590
And hence, agent safety and security is much more complex.

129
00:08:36,590 --> 00:08:40,120
And also, the consequence can be much more severe.

130
00:08:40,120 --> 00:08:44,169
So given the interest of time, again, I

131
00:08:44,169 --> 00:08:46,570
would encourage you to learn more

132
00:08:46,570 --> 00:08:52,990
about the safety and security issues and various mitigation

133
00:08:52,990 --> 00:08:59,410
and defense by watching my ICLR keynote.

134
00:08:59,410 --> 00:09:02,440
And in this lecture, we will focus

135
00:09:02,440 --> 00:09:07,240
more on the more complex issues of LLM agent safety

136
00:09:07,240 --> 00:09:13,420
and security, which, of course, will

137
00:09:13,420 --> 00:09:16,840
be built on some of the foundation issues

138
00:09:16,840 --> 00:09:20,295
with safety and security issues of LLM as well.

139
00:09:23,140 --> 00:09:27,940
OK, so now, so first, let's just revisit

140
00:09:27,940 --> 00:09:34,430
what is a LLM agent and also an agentic AI system.

141
00:09:34,430 --> 00:09:38,020
So again, as I mentioned, so with the LLM agent,

142
00:09:38,020 --> 00:09:42,220
essentially we have the LLM model as a core,

143
00:09:42,220 --> 00:09:49,600
but it interacts with the environment and also--

144
00:09:49,600 --> 00:09:50,600
and the user.

145
00:09:50,600 --> 00:09:56,170
And it can take queries from the user

146
00:09:56,170 --> 00:10:00,160
and also observe from the environment.

147
00:10:00,160 --> 00:10:04,240
And then it can use its reasoning and planning

148
00:10:04,240 --> 00:10:10,180
to plan its actions to further retrieve data

149
00:10:10,180 --> 00:10:14,380
from the external database and use various tools,

150
00:10:14,380 --> 00:10:17,200
and so on, and then take actions in the environment.

151
00:10:17,200 --> 00:10:26,350
And we can essentially simplify the agentic AI system

152
00:10:26,350 --> 00:10:32,770
as the following architecture, with, again, the user

153
00:10:32,770 --> 00:10:36,520
to interact with agentic system, where users can submit queries

154
00:10:36,520 --> 00:10:39,340
to ask the agent, for example, to perform certain tasks

155
00:10:39,340 --> 00:10:40,810
on behalf of the user.

156
00:10:40,810 --> 00:10:44,350
And also the, well, the agentic system

157
00:10:44,350 --> 00:10:46,690
will interact with the external world.

158
00:10:46,690 --> 00:10:49,960
And also the LLM here can be multi-modal.

159
00:10:49,960 --> 00:10:53,740
It can take actually various types of multi-modal inputs

160
00:10:53,740 --> 00:10:58,853
and also can produce different types of outputs.

161
00:11:01,420 --> 00:11:06,100
And here, I also want to contrast this agentic system

162
00:11:06,100 --> 00:11:08,260
to traditional system.

163
00:11:08,260 --> 00:11:11,780
So in the traditional computer system,

164
00:11:11,780 --> 00:11:18,280
we essentially have what I call a symbolic programs, which

165
00:11:18,280 --> 00:11:21,280
is, for example, what we typically use,

166
00:11:21,280 --> 00:11:26,450
like operating system the web browser, mobile applications.

167
00:11:26,450 --> 00:11:28,690
So these are traditional system composed

168
00:11:28,690 --> 00:11:35,860
of these symbolic components, like program functions,

169
00:11:35,860 --> 00:11:37,940
and so on.

170
00:11:37,940 --> 00:11:40,030
And then in this case, also, the user

171
00:11:40,030 --> 00:11:43,360
can interact with the traditional system,

172
00:11:43,360 --> 00:11:45,580
and the traditional system can also

173
00:11:45,580 --> 00:11:49,000
interact with the external world as well.

174
00:11:49,000 --> 00:11:52,460
So now with the agentic AI, this agentic system,

175
00:11:52,460 --> 00:11:56,090
we call it a hybrid or compound system.

176
00:11:56,090 --> 00:12:00,370
So here, in addition to the traditional symbolic components

177
00:12:00,370 --> 00:12:05,290
that we have here, we also have these, I call, non-symbolic,

178
00:12:05,290 --> 00:12:09,040
for example, neurocomponents, like large language models,

179
00:12:09,040 --> 00:12:11,380
and so on, which will actually help

180
00:12:11,380 --> 00:12:14,860
provide certain intelligence in the system.

181
00:12:14,860 --> 00:12:19,420
And so, of course, within a hybrid system,

182
00:12:19,420 --> 00:12:22,060
you can have multiple--

183
00:12:22,060 --> 00:12:24,640
just like you can have multiple symbolic components

184
00:12:24,640 --> 00:12:25,940
in the traditional system.

185
00:12:25,940 --> 00:12:27,910
In the hybrid system, besides having

186
00:12:27,910 --> 00:12:29,680
multiple symbolic components, you

187
00:12:29,680 --> 00:12:31,910
can also have multiple neural components.

188
00:12:31,910 --> 00:12:35,600
You can have, for example, different models,

189
00:12:35,600 --> 00:12:40,590
and you can have different m and so on.

190
00:12:40,590 --> 00:12:47,340
So, as you can see, a agentic AI system is much more complex.

191
00:12:47,340 --> 00:12:50,090
And it's a hybrid system that contains

192
00:12:50,090 --> 00:12:53,630
both symbolic and neural components,

193
00:12:53,630 --> 00:12:57,410
and can contain multiple symbolic and multiple neural

194
00:12:57,410 --> 00:12:59,540
components.

195
00:12:59,540 --> 00:13:01,880
So here is an example walkthrough

196
00:13:01,880 --> 00:13:06,590
of an agentic hybrid system workflow.

197
00:13:06,590 --> 00:13:11,670
So here in the first step, for example,

198
00:13:11,670 --> 00:13:14,720
a host, like a developer, can prepare

199
00:13:14,720 --> 00:13:17,570
the model in the hybrid system and then

200
00:13:17,570 --> 00:13:21,440
deploy the hybrid system here with the model--

201
00:13:21,440 --> 00:13:22,820
multiple models.

202
00:13:22,820 --> 00:13:25,020
And then, once the system is deployed,

203
00:13:25,020 --> 00:13:27,390
the user can interact with the system.

204
00:13:27,390 --> 00:13:32,420
The user can, for example, send the requests--

205
00:13:32,420 --> 00:13:34,560
the user can send requests to the system.

206
00:13:34,560 --> 00:13:41,550
And then the system can process the requests and then prepare--

207
00:13:41,550 --> 00:13:45,380
assemble the prompts, and then invoke the model

208
00:13:45,380 --> 00:13:47,660
with the assembled prompts.

209
00:13:47,660 --> 00:13:50,720
The prompt then interacts with the rest of the system

210
00:13:50,720 --> 00:13:52,980
by generating the outputs.

211
00:13:52,980 --> 00:13:57,050
And then the rest of the system may further process the outputs.

212
00:13:57,050 --> 00:14:01,490
And then the system can also then interact

213
00:14:01,490 --> 00:14:06,450
with the external world from the final generated output

214
00:14:06,450 --> 00:14:11,580
and can take the final actions on the external world as well.

215
00:14:11,580 --> 00:14:18,050
And also, the system can respond to the user

216
00:14:18,050 --> 00:14:20,100
as well, to the user's query.

217
00:14:20,100 --> 00:14:23,000
And overall, the system can continuously

218
00:14:23,000 --> 00:14:27,050
run for long-term tasks.

219
00:14:27,050 --> 00:14:27,780
Right.

220
00:14:27,780 --> 00:14:32,450
So this is an example workflow and walkthrough

221
00:14:32,450 --> 00:14:34,105
of an agentic hybrid system.

222
00:14:36,770 --> 00:14:41,750
And also, this hybrid agentic system sometimes

223
00:14:41,750 --> 00:14:45,590
can also, of course, interact with another hybrid system,

224
00:14:45,590 --> 00:14:50,240
another agent, forming multi-agent communication

225
00:14:50,240 --> 00:14:54,260
and interaction as well.

226
00:14:54,260 --> 00:14:58,070
So when we look at this agentic hybrid system,

227
00:14:58,070 --> 00:15:02,750
it has a set of security and safety goals.

228
00:15:02,750 --> 00:15:05,990
For example, for the security goals,

229
00:15:05,990 --> 00:15:11,540
it's similar to-- at a high it's similar to the standard set

230
00:15:11,540 --> 00:15:15,990
of security goals that we call CIA, confidentiality, security,

231
00:15:15,990 --> 00:15:17,720
and availability.

232
00:15:17,720 --> 00:15:21,200
So for confidentiality, it needs to ensure that information

233
00:15:21,200 --> 00:15:26,750
is accessible only to those authorized entities,

234
00:15:26,750 --> 00:15:30,140
and this includes-- the sensitive information can

235
00:15:30,140 --> 00:15:33,740
include system secrets, user credentials, user data,

236
00:15:33,740 --> 00:15:36,590
and model itself, and so on.

237
00:15:36,590 --> 00:15:42,220
And for integrity in the overall agentic hybrid system,

238
00:15:42,220 --> 00:15:44,840
you need to ensure that the system and the data

239
00:15:44,840 --> 00:15:49,160
has not been altered or tampered with, either intentionally

240
00:15:49,160 --> 00:15:54,080
or accidentally, and remains accurate and trustworthy.

241
00:15:54,080 --> 00:15:57,620
And for availability, and to ensure that the authorized users

242
00:15:57,620 --> 00:16:02,570
have reliable and timely access to data systems and services

243
00:16:02,570 --> 00:16:04,250
and resources.

244
00:16:04,250 --> 00:16:07,700
So this is for the security side.

245
00:16:07,700 --> 00:16:12,740
Again, as I mentioned earlier, the security goals

246
00:16:12,740 --> 00:16:15,110
for agentic hybrid system here is

247
00:16:15,110 --> 00:16:20,420
need to protect the system itself from attacks and exploits

248
00:16:20,420 --> 00:16:25,970
from external, malicious attackers,

249
00:16:25,970 --> 00:16:28,320
and that's outside of the system.

250
00:16:28,320 --> 00:16:31,820
And for the safety goals, essentially

251
00:16:31,820 --> 00:16:35,840
to ensure that the agentic hybrid system does not

252
00:16:35,840 --> 00:16:37,710
harm the external world.

253
00:16:37,710 --> 00:16:45,260
So, for example, we don't want the agentic system,

254
00:16:45,260 --> 00:16:50,480
for example, agents to launch attacks on the internet

255
00:16:50,480 --> 00:16:55,530
to other, for example, potential vulnerable systems' websites.

256
00:16:55,530 --> 00:17:02,450
And also we want to help avoid collisions

257
00:17:02,450 --> 00:17:03,950
for self-driving cars.

258
00:17:03,950 --> 00:17:08,150
And medical systems should not misdiagnose

259
00:17:08,150 --> 00:17:11,810
in ways that endanger patients, and so on.

260
00:17:11,810 --> 00:17:18,170
So overall, we need to ensure the agentic hybrid system

261
00:17:18,170 --> 00:17:21,380
not to have harmful consequences during,

262
00:17:21,380 --> 00:17:24,859
either normal operations, edge cases, failure modes,

263
00:17:24,859 --> 00:17:26,185
or under attacks.

264
00:17:29,240 --> 00:17:33,110
So then, how do we compare, for example, the security

265
00:17:33,110 --> 00:17:38,130
goals of agentic hybrid system versus traditional system?

266
00:17:38,130 --> 00:17:45,000
So one aspect of the differences is that for agentic AI system

267
00:17:45,000 --> 00:17:47,970
it has additional targets that we need to protect.

268
00:17:47,970 --> 00:17:50,690
So, for example, for confidentiality.

269
00:17:50,690 --> 00:17:56,720
of agentic AI system we need to also, besides traditional, like,

270
00:17:56,720 --> 00:18:00,030
user data in the traditional computer system,

271
00:18:00,030 --> 00:18:03,920
we also need to protect sensitive information related

272
00:18:03,920 --> 00:18:07,590
to the neural components like the models

273
00:18:07,590 --> 00:18:13,620
and also include, for example, API keys for inference services,

274
00:18:13,620 --> 00:18:18,890
secret prompts like the input from users related

275
00:18:18,890 --> 00:18:22,040
to the prompts and the interaction

276
00:18:22,040 --> 00:18:26,330
history, the proprietary model parameters, and so on.

277
00:18:26,330 --> 00:18:31,800
And for integrity, again, besides the integrity

278
00:18:31,800 --> 00:18:37,150
for traditional data in traditional system,

279
00:18:37,150 --> 00:18:40,240
here, we also need to ensure integrity related to the model.

280
00:18:40,240 --> 00:18:41,850
For example, model integrity.

281
00:18:41,850 --> 00:18:45,630
And for availability, similarly, we need to, in addition,

282
00:18:45,630 --> 00:18:49,270
ensure model performance and service availability.

283
00:18:55,860 --> 00:19:01,230
Also, when we look at the agentic hybrid system

284
00:19:01,230 --> 00:19:03,450
versus the traditional system, we

285
00:19:03,450 --> 00:19:06,300
can also see that given the complexity of the system,

286
00:19:06,300 --> 00:19:11,940
and also given, in particular, the use of the large language

287
00:19:11,940 --> 00:19:16,740
models, there is actually increased attack

288
00:19:16,740 --> 00:19:20,290
surface due to the use of large language models.

289
00:19:20,290 --> 00:19:27,360
So, for example, for these three key security properties,

290
00:19:27,360 --> 00:19:34,020
the use of LLM introduces increased attack surface.

291
00:19:34,020 --> 00:19:38,040
For example, for confidentiality, the LLM

292
00:19:38,040 --> 00:19:40,240
can process sensitive information.

293
00:19:40,240 --> 00:19:43,530
And then when it reveals--

294
00:19:43,530 --> 00:19:49,270
when it sends outputs to either to the user or to, for example,

295
00:19:49,270 --> 00:19:53,940
the external world through in the ends, the overall system

296
00:19:53,940 --> 00:19:57,150
outputs, this output actually could

297
00:19:57,150 --> 00:20:00,390
contain sensitive information.

298
00:20:00,390 --> 00:20:03,240
So this is an additional attack surface

299
00:20:03,240 --> 00:20:06,870
due to the use of large language models.

300
00:20:06,870 --> 00:20:13,800
And secondly, for integrity, because the LLM can--

301
00:20:13,800 --> 00:20:18,300
the prompt to the LLM can utilize untrusted data,

302
00:20:18,300 --> 00:20:21,340
either from the user or from external world.

303
00:20:21,340 --> 00:20:28,290
And also, the model itself may be from the external world,

304
00:20:28,290 --> 00:20:30,730
from a supply chain.

305
00:20:30,730 --> 00:20:35,250
And all these can be from untrusted sources

306
00:20:35,250 --> 00:20:41,970
and can cause the model essentially to misbehave.

307
00:20:41,970 --> 00:20:44,610
For example, through poisoning, data

308
00:20:44,610 --> 00:20:48,630
contamination due to supply chain attacks.

309
00:20:48,630 --> 00:20:55,740
The model itself may be poisoned or contain children, and so on.

310
00:20:55,740 --> 00:20:59,790
And then, finally, there can be various denial

311
00:20:59,790 --> 00:21:03,120
of service attacks on the model itself

312
00:21:03,120 --> 00:21:09,960
as well, impacting availability of the overall system.

313
00:21:09,960 --> 00:21:15,460
So that's an overview of agentic AI safety and security.

314
00:21:15,460 --> 00:21:19,410
And as we can see, given the increased complexity

315
00:21:19,410 --> 00:21:28,290
of the overall agentic hybrid system and also the use of LLM,

316
00:21:28,290 --> 00:21:34,080
both the system itself and also the security and safety goals

317
00:21:34,080 --> 00:21:36,690
become more complex, and also there

318
00:21:36,690 --> 00:21:39,310
is increased attack surface.

319
00:21:39,310 --> 00:21:43,050
So now let's go into more details

320
00:21:43,050 --> 00:21:47,670
to look at how these increase attack

321
00:21:47,670 --> 00:21:52,860
surfaces can manifest in different types of attacks

322
00:21:52,860 --> 00:21:55,500
in agentic AI.

323
00:21:55,500 --> 00:21:58,930
So first, now let's take a deeper dive.

324
00:21:58,930 --> 00:22:03,160
Look at what could go wrong in agentic hybrid system.

325
00:22:03,160 --> 00:22:05,670
So again, let's walk--

326
00:22:05,670 --> 00:22:08,220
let's follow the walkthrough that we did earlier

327
00:22:08,220 --> 00:22:11,590
through this hybrid system.

328
00:22:11,590 --> 00:22:15,960
And then we can look at each step, what could go wrong.

329
00:22:15,960 --> 00:22:21,870
So the first step is that the host hosts or the developer

330
00:22:21,870 --> 00:22:26,160
prepares the model and system and then deploy

331
00:22:26,160 --> 00:22:27,640
the overall system.

332
00:22:27,640 --> 00:22:31,660
So in this case, what could go wrong?

333
00:22:31,660 --> 00:22:34,780
So, for example, as I already mentioned,

334
00:22:34,780 --> 00:22:39,750
the model itself actually can be flawed.

335
00:22:39,750 --> 00:22:43,990
For example, due to supply chain attacks,

336
00:22:43,990 --> 00:22:47,970
the model itself could actually contain malicious code,

337
00:22:47,970 --> 00:22:53,610
could contain poisoned backdoor, and also

338
00:22:53,610 --> 00:22:57,780
contain other types of vulnerabilities, and so on.

339
00:22:57,780 --> 00:22:59,560
So that's the first step.

340
00:22:59,560 --> 00:23:01,980
Now, when-- in the second step, user

341
00:23:01,980 --> 00:23:03,850
sends requests to the system.

342
00:23:03,850 --> 00:23:09,240
So in certain cases, the user request could be malicious

343
00:23:09,240 --> 00:23:11,700
or could contain untrusted data, given

344
00:23:11,700 --> 00:23:16,920
that user requests may use data from untrusted sources as well.

345
00:23:16,920 --> 00:23:22,210
And then the third step, the system will process the requests

346
00:23:22,210 --> 00:23:28,450
and then assemble the prompts and then invoke the model.

347
00:23:28,450 --> 00:23:32,830
So in this case, given that the inputs

348
00:23:32,830 --> 00:23:35,110
may contain untrusted data, there

349
00:23:35,110 --> 00:23:40,400
could be issues that there may be insufficient input

350
00:23:40,400 --> 00:23:44,410
sanitization or validation, such that the assembled

351
00:23:44,410 --> 00:23:50,860
prompts to the LLM still contain untrusted data that can further

352
00:23:50,860 --> 00:23:54,610
cause LLM to misbehave.

353
00:23:54,610 --> 00:23:59,230
And now, as the model interacts with the rest of the system

354
00:23:59,230 --> 00:24:04,420
by generating outputs and also take actions to various function

355
00:24:04,420 --> 00:24:13,000
calls and so on, the generated output could also be malicious

356
00:24:13,000 --> 00:24:20,390
or the outputs could take the wrong action,

357
00:24:20,390 --> 00:24:24,130
then this can cause further attacks

358
00:24:24,130 --> 00:24:26,920
on the rest of the system.

359
00:24:26,920 --> 00:24:31,150
So this either could be because the LLM has been attacked

360
00:24:31,150 --> 00:24:36,940
due to malicious inputs, or the LLM simply just misbehaves

361
00:24:36,940 --> 00:24:46,390
given LLM's own unreliability or of other vulnerability

362
00:24:46,390 --> 00:24:48,520
and so on.

363
00:24:48,520 --> 00:24:55,720
And the next step, the system can then utilize the output

364
00:24:55,720 --> 00:24:59,300
from LLM to then further take actions,

365
00:24:59,300 --> 00:25:03,050
produce outputs, to interact with the external world.

366
00:25:03,050 --> 00:25:11,110
And then again, in this case, given that the system's--

367
00:25:11,110 --> 00:25:15,430
the assembled outputs or action could be malicious,

368
00:25:15,430 --> 00:25:18,220
could be wrong behaviors, and this

369
00:25:18,220 --> 00:25:22,330
can result in further attacks or additional harms

370
00:25:22,330 --> 00:25:25,960
to the external world.

371
00:25:25,960 --> 00:25:29,930
And then also similarly, as the system responds to the user,

372
00:25:29,930 --> 00:25:31,900
the system output could also harm

373
00:25:31,900 --> 00:25:35,780
the user in various different ways,

374
00:25:35,780 --> 00:25:39,520
either producing toxic outputs to the user

375
00:25:39,520 --> 00:25:45,850
or even taking other actions that could harm the user.

376
00:25:45,850 --> 00:25:50,680
And finally, the system is supposed to continuously run

377
00:25:50,680 --> 00:25:53,770
for long-term tasks, but however, there

378
00:25:53,770 --> 00:25:59,800
can be very resource issues or other denial of service attacks

379
00:25:59,800 --> 00:26:03,560
that cause the system to become unavailable.

380
00:26:03,560 --> 00:26:09,110
So, as you can see with each step in this walkthrough,

381
00:26:09,110 --> 00:26:13,210
there can be places, attack surfaces

382
00:26:13,210 --> 00:26:17,770
where untrusted data and malicious data that

383
00:26:17,770 --> 00:26:19,340
can be introduced.

384
00:26:19,340 --> 00:26:22,690
And also, as the system overall interacts

385
00:26:22,690 --> 00:26:24,860
with the external world and with the user,

386
00:26:24,860 --> 00:26:32,590
the outputs can potentially harm the external world, and so on.

387
00:26:32,590 --> 00:26:36,220
So here we focus on--

388
00:26:36,220 --> 00:26:38,090
with the hybrid system, in particular,

389
00:26:38,090 --> 00:26:40,900
we focus on attacks related to large language

390
00:26:40,900 --> 00:26:44,510
models in contrast to traditional systems.

391
00:26:44,510 --> 00:26:48,940
And in particular, given that the large language model here

392
00:26:48,940 --> 00:26:53,530
is used as intelligence in the system,

393
00:26:53,530 --> 00:26:56,950
when taking various inputs, it can produce

394
00:26:56,950 --> 00:26:59,270
different types of outputs.

395
00:26:59,270 --> 00:27:02,980
And the LLM generated outputs essentially

396
00:27:02,980 --> 00:27:06,580
can then be used as part of the overall attack chain

397
00:27:06,580 --> 00:27:10,580
to enable various attacks to the rest of the system,

398
00:27:10,580 --> 00:27:15,700
as well as, in the end, to the external world to cause harm.

399
00:27:15,700 --> 00:27:22,240
So here are some examples, how LLM-generated output can be used

400
00:27:22,240 --> 00:27:24,820
as part of the attack chain.

401
00:27:24,820 --> 00:27:29,420
So again, the LLM can generate different types of outputs.

402
00:27:29,420 --> 00:27:33,490
So the outputs essentially will be used in different ways.

403
00:27:33,490 --> 00:27:39,250
So, for example, the LLM can generate user external-facing

404
00:27:39,250 --> 00:27:43,840
outputs in different modalities, such as text and image,

405
00:27:43,840 --> 00:27:44,660
and so on.

406
00:27:44,660 --> 00:27:47,330
And this could lead to information leakage.

407
00:27:47,330 --> 00:27:51,880
And also the produced outputs, again, could be toxic,

408
00:27:51,880 --> 00:27:57,190
could contain, like, dangerous information,

409
00:27:57,190 --> 00:28:00,260
such as how to instruction, how to make a bomb,

410
00:28:00,260 --> 00:28:05,950
how to launch other attacks, and so on.

411
00:28:05,950 --> 00:28:12,590
And so many such potential secret and privacy

412
00:28:12,590 --> 00:28:16,460
challenges and also safety harm.

413
00:28:16,460 --> 00:28:25,310
And the LLM outputs also can be used for further model

414
00:28:25,310 --> 00:28:29,840
invocation and computation in the rest of the hybrid system

415
00:28:29,840 --> 00:28:30,660
as well.

416
00:28:30,660 --> 00:28:35,060
So, in this case, issues in the outputs

417
00:28:35,060 --> 00:28:41,330
could lead to compounding bias and errors

418
00:28:41,330 --> 00:28:45,710
in the rest of the system in further computation.

419
00:28:45,710 --> 00:28:48,690
And also, the LLM outputs could be used,

420
00:28:48,690 --> 00:28:52,460
could be interpreted as branch or jump conditions

421
00:28:52,460 --> 00:28:57,330
to alter the control flow of the rest of the system.

422
00:28:57,330 --> 00:29:02,300
And in this case, issues in LLM-generated output

423
00:29:02,300 --> 00:29:06,920
could lead to unexpected system behavior.

424
00:29:06,920 --> 00:29:10,830
And also, as we know, as part of the agents,

425
00:29:10,830 --> 00:29:17,540
the LLM can produce outputs to enable--

426
00:29:17,540 --> 00:29:19,980
invoke further function calls.

427
00:29:19,980 --> 00:29:23,060
So by outputting, for example, parameters for the function

428
00:29:23,060 --> 00:29:28,080
calls and also the name of the function on the tool, and so on.

429
00:29:28,080 --> 00:29:31,950
So we are going to see some concrete examples of this.

430
00:29:31,950 --> 00:29:38,690
So when LLM-generated outputs are being used directly or even

431
00:29:38,690 --> 00:29:42,710
after insufficient processing as parameters to certain function

432
00:29:42,710 --> 00:29:45,740
calls to use, it can further lead

433
00:29:45,740 --> 00:29:51,440
to security vulnerabilities, such as SQL injection,

434
00:29:51,440 --> 00:29:54,690
and server-side request forgery, SSRF, and so on.

435
00:29:54,690 --> 00:29:59,900
And we'll see some more detailed examples of this in a minute.

436
00:29:59,900 --> 00:30:05,150
And also, sometimes LLM will be used to directly generate code,

437
00:30:05,150 --> 00:30:09,810
and then this code can be executed in the system,

438
00:30:09,810 --> 00:30:16,160
and this one LLM generates the outputs, in this case, code,

439
00:30:16,160 --> 00:30:17,550
has issues.

440
00:30:17,550 --> 00:30:22,010
So, for example, it can actually generate malicious code.

441
00:30:22,010 --> 00:30:27,990
And when under attack, LLM somehow misbehaves.

442
00:30:27,990 --> 00:30:30,710
This could lead to security vulnerabilities,

443
00:30:30,710 --> 00:30:34,640
such as arbitrary code execution.

444
00:30:34,640 --> 00:30:39,450
So again, as we can see, now, given this LLM component,

445
00:30:39,450 --> 00:30:42,110
which is very complex, we do not really

446
00:30:42,110 --> 00:30:45,000
understand how this LLM works.

447
00:30:45,000 --> 00:30:48,350
And it's generally the outputs can

448
00:30:48,350 --> 00:30:51,110
be used in many different ways and can

449
00:30:51,110 --> 00:30:54,620
have huge impacts on the rest of the system,

450
00:30:54,620 --> 00:30:58,340
can alter the control flow of the system,

451
00:30:58,340 --> 00:31:03,170
and can even introduce new code into the system.

452
00:31:03,170 --> 00:31:06,650
All this LLM-generated output can then

453
00:31:06,650 --> 00:31:11,150
be used as part of the attack chain.

454
00:31:11,150 --> 00:31:14,390
So overall, I also wanted to briefly introduce

455
00:31:14,390 --> 00:31:19,070
how we consider the model's safety and security levels.

456
00:31:19,070 --> 00:31:24,930
So here, we can classify it into several different levels.

457
00:31:24,930 --> 00:31:30,080
So ideally, we want the model to be perfect in the sense

458
00:31:30,080 --> 00:31:35,450
that it's accurate, has essentially perfect utility

459
00:31:35,450 --> 00:31:37,650
performance, and perfect security.

460
00:31:37,650 --> 00:31:39,720
So it's secure against all attacks.

461
00:31:39,720 --> 00:31:41,280
So, of course, this is a Holy Grail.

462
00:31:41,280 --> 00:31:43,320
This is what we are aiming for.

463
00:31:43,320 --> 00:31:47,240
But, of course, it's extremely difficult to achieve.

464
00:31:47,240 --> 00:31:52,200
So the next level is the model is accurate but vulnerable.

465
00:31:52,200 --> 00:31:53,870
So essentially, in general, the model

466
00:31:53,870 --> 00:31:57,050
gives-- under normal circumstance it

467
00:31:57,050 --> 00:32:00,090
will give the accurate output.

468
00:32:00,090 --> 00:32:06,120
But however, it's not trained for defending against attacks,

469
00:32:06,120 --> 00:32:10,820
so it's vulnerable to different types of prompt engineering

470
00:32:10,820 --> 00:32:13,260
attacks, including prompt injection, jailbreak,

471
00:32:13,260 --> 00:32:16,900
adversarial examples, and also prompt leakage, and so on.

472
00:32:19,520 --> 00:32:28,590
So next level is the model is inaccurate and vulnerable.

473
00:32:28,590 --> 00:32:33,350
So in addition to not being secure against attacks,

474
00:32:33,350 --> 00:32:39,560
it also is inaccurate, which is typically the case.

475
00:32:39,560 --> 00:32:42,180
As we know, models often hallucinate.

476
00:32:42,180 --> 00:32:47,460
It has many robustness issues and other issues.

477
00:32:47,460 --> 00:32:58,280
So they-- so in this case, in addition to safety and security

478
00:32:58,280 --> 00:33:02,760
issues mentioned above, also in addition, the model

479
00:33:02,760 --> 00:33:06,510
is vulnerable to, for example, misbehaviors

480
00:33:06,510 --> 00:33:11,430
such as hallucination caused unexpected behaviors as well.

481
00:33:11,430 --> 00:33:14,610
And then the next level.

482
00:33:14,610 --> 00:33:19,360
Not only that the model is inaccurate and vulnerable,

483
00:33:19,360 --> 00:33:22,540
but also it actually can be poisoned,

484
00:33:22,540 --> 00:33:26,400
which means it can contain undesired behaviors

485
00:33:26,400 --> 00:33:27,790
under certain--

486
00:33:27,790 --> 00:33:30,653
even seemingly normal-looking inputs.

487
00:33:33,930 --> 00:33:36,400
So essentially-- So, for example,

488
00:33:36,400 --> 00:33:39,130
in this case, in addition to the above issues,

489
00:33:39,130 --> 00:33:42,810
the model could also be vulnerable to backdoors

490
00:33:42,810 --> 00:33:47,340
embedded in the poisoned model.

491
00:33:47,340 --> 00:33:53,910
And finally, the model could just be completely malicious.

492
00:33:53,910 --> 00:33:58,800
For example, due to supply chain attacks

493
00:33:58,800 --> 00:34:02,790
the model contains malware, it's intentionally

494
00:34:02,790 --> 00:34:07,180
designed to be a malicious model to cause harm.

495
00:34:07,180 --> 00:34:11,110
And so, in addition to the above potential issues,

496
00:34:11,110 --> 00:34:16,590
it can be also vulnerable to model loading-based,

497
00:34:16,590 --> 00:34:18,670
for example, remote code execution.

498
00:34:18,670 --> 00:34:21,239
So as soon as you load the model,

499
00:34:21,239 --> 00:34:27,659
the model actually could cause remote code execution-type

500
00:34:27,659 --> 00:34:33,120
of vulnerabilities and exploits.

501
00:34:33,120 --> 00:34:35,800
So as we develop agents, of course,

502
00:34:35,800 --> 00:34:40,230
we want the model to be as good as possible

503
00:34:40,230 --> 00:34:44,949
to have as high as possible safety and security levels.

504
00:34:44,949 --> 00:34:47,969
But however, we do need to develop

505
00:34:47,969 --> 00:34:53,070
a defense-in-depth solutions to defend against cases where

506
00:34:53,070 --> 00:34:56,790
the model's safety and security levels are even

507
00:34:56,790 --> 00:34:59,420
lower than perfect.

508
00:35:04,140 --> 00:35:08,460
So we also want to talk a little bit about not only

509
00:35:08,460 --> 00:35:11,910
that models and systems can be attacked,

510
00:35:11,910 --> 00:35:16,720
but also the models and systems can be misused as well.

511
00:35:16,720 --> 00:35:23,800
And the essentially agent misuse can harm both the victim system,

512
00:35:23,800 --> 00:35:28,260
the system itself, as well as the external systems.

513
00:35:28,260 --> 00:35:30,630
And also, when we look at this, we

514
00:35:30,630 --> 00:35:33,340
can look at misuse at both levels.

515
00:35:33,340 --> 00:35:37,230
The model misuse and system misuse.

516
00:35:37,230 --> 00:35:41,130
So model misuse is even when the model, when you just

517
00:35:41,130 --> 00:35:44,700
consider its inputs and outputs in the simple setting, the model

518
00:35:44,700 --> 00:35:47,640
can be misused, for example, to generate

519
00:35:47,640 --> 00:35:50,140
various undesired, unsafe outputs,

520
00:35:50,140 --> 00:35:55,110
including generating malware code, generating bomb creation

521
00:35:55,110 --> 00:35:56,700
instructions.

